# Memoization

`memo`, `useMemo`, and `useCallback` are performance optimizations. The screen must behave the same without them. When the React Compiler is enabled, leave them out: the compiler already caches components, values, and handlers.

`memo` skips a re-render when every prop is `Object.is` to the previous prop. Use it when those props stay the same while the parent re-renders. A table head, a sidebar, and a top bar with no props are that case. Skip it when a prop is a new function or a new element on every render. Skip the second argument: a custom comparison has to check every prop, including functions, and a deep compare can freeze the page.

Do not cache JSX with `useMemo`. That block is a component in its own file. The parent renders `<StreamTableHead />`. It does not wrap an already memoized child in `useMemo`.

`useMemo` caches a calculation. Use it when the value is a prop of a `memo` component, such as a mapped list passed to a memo table, or when another hook depends on that value. A context value is the last case: a new object on each render updates every reader. Mapping a list is not slow by itself.

`useCallback` caches a function. It is `useMemo(() => fn, deps)` and nothing more. Use it when that function is a prop of a `memo` component or a dependency of another hook. When the handler only writes the next state, pass an updater so the function has no dependency: `setTheme((theme) => (theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark'))`.

A wrapper that accepts `children` already keeps those children from re-rendering when its own state changes. Keep form state in the form.

```tsx
// Bad: memo never hits, because onEdit is a new function on every keystroke
export const UserTable = memo(function UserTable({ rows, onEdit }: Props) {
  return null;
});

<UserTable rows={rows} onEdit={(id) => edit(id)} />

// Good: event handler is a memoized function so it skips a render when the id is the same
export const UserTable = memo(function UserTable({ rows, onEdit }: Props) {
  return null;
});

const onEdit = useCallback((id: string) => edit(id), [edit]);

<UserTable rows={rows} onEdit={onEdit} />
```
